    "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( f(x) = x(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3) \\), we can start by rewriting the expression in a more convenient form. Notice that:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = x(x+3)(x+1)(x+2) = (x^2 + 3x)(x^2 + 3x + 2)\n\\]\n\nLet \\( y = x^2 + 3x \\). Then the function becomes:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = y(y + 2) = y^2 + 2y\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the minimum value of the quadratic function \\( g(y) = y^2 + 2y \\). This is a parabola that opens upwards, so its minimum value occurs at the vertex. The vertex of a parabola \\( ay^2 + by + c \\) is given by \\( y = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\). Here, \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 2 \\), so:\n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot 1} = -1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( y = -1 \\) into the function \\( g(y) \\):\n\n\\[\ng(-1) = (-1)^2 + 2(-1) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( y^2 + 2y \\) is \\(-1\\). Now we need to determine if there is a real value of \\( x \\) such that \\( y = x^2 + 3x = -1 \\). We solve the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nUsing the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) with \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe solutions are \\( x = \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{-3 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\). Therefore, the minimum value of the original function \\( f(x) \\) is indeed \\(-1\\).\n\nThe minimum value of \\( x(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
